# November 20, 2026: a first statewide scatter across Minnesota, region mn, taken by tools/choose.py --thinnest (Minnesota
# had 15 places, 14 of them in Washington County). Places spread from Moorhead and Pipestone to Duluth, the North Shore,
# the Iron Range, the Minnesota River valley and the bluff country of the southeast.
# Facts come from sources opened in this session: Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py) and its Register list rows,
# National Register nomination forms read with page.py (Kettle River Sandstone Company Quarry, 91000877; Pipestone
# Commercial Historic District, 77000761), Dodge County's own news page, a KTTC news report of July 28, 2026, and the
# managing bodies' own pages. The Minnesota Historical Society's and Minnesota DNR's pages are drawn by script: page.py
# reads them empty, so they were read in a headless browser (Playwright) on November 20, 2026, and the hours below are
# what those pages showed that day. Glensheen's and the Pickwick Mill's hours are from their own sites the same day.
# Brick values: from the sources' words about what each place is built of, or a picture opened with tools/brick.py look:
# 'Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg' (tower and buildings of yellow-buff brick: no), 'Glensheen.JPG' (red brick walls and
# terrace: yes), 'Folsom House 2015.jpg' (white clapboard house with a red brick chimney: some). '?' where none settled it.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'mn_axba','label':'Minnesota: a first scatter across the state','added':'2026-11-20 07:24'}
NOM=lambda ref,label: [label,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/'+ref+'_text']
MNHS=lambda path,label: ['MNHS: '+label,'https://www.mnhs.org/'+path]
DNR=lambda slug,label: ['Minnesota DNR: '+label,'https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_parks/'+slug+'/index.html']
PLACES=[
# ---- North Shore, Duluth and the Iron Range ----
N('split-rock-lighthouse','Split Rock Lighthouse','Beaver Bay Township','mn','light','standing',47.20005,-91.3669,'exact','open','walk','','1910–1969',
  'Built by the United States Lighthouse Service after the Mataafa Storm of 1905, in which 29 ships were lost or damaged on Lake Superior, and finished in 1910 for $75,000 with its buildings and land. The light stands on a sheer cliff of about 133 feet. It was put out of service in 1969, the year the state park was set up round it; it is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'An octagonal tower, steel-framed with brick walls and concrete trim on a concrete foundation set into the cliff rock, topped by a steel lantern that still holds its third-order bi-valve Fresnel lens made by Barbier, Bernard and Turenne of Paris (Wikipedia). MNHS says the buildings and grounds, the visitor centre, the fog signal building and the lighthouse are open only during site hours; in winter 2026 a grounds pass did not include the lighthouse or keeper\'s house, which were shown on a booked Keeper\'s Tour. The wreck of the Madeira lies just north of the light (Wikipedia).',
  'The lighthouse a storm built, on a 133-foot cliff over Lake Superior.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society site inside Split Rock Lighthouse State Park, 3713 Split Rock Lighthouse Road, off Highway 61 southwest of Silver Bay. Free parking for site visitors during site hours; a state park vehicle permit is needed at other times. No pets on the historic site. Hours from the MNHS page, read November 20, 2026 (they change with the season; summer hours not read).',
  links=[W('Split Rock Lighthouse'),MNHS('splitrock','Split Rock Lighthouse, hours and admission'),MNHS('splitrock/visit','plan your visit')],
  hours='October 20, 2026 to January 4, 2027: Thursday to Monday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Keeper\'s and Christmas tours booked separately',brick='no'),
N('glensheen-duluth','Glensheen, the Congdon estate','Duluth','mn','inst','standing',46.81518,-92.05175,'site','open','walk','','1905–1908',
  'A Jacobean mansion of 39 rooms built from 1905 to 1908 for Chester Adgate Congdon, whose fortune came from iron mining, on 12 acres of Lake Superior shore. Clarence H. Johnston Sr. designed the house, the William A. French Company the interiors, and the Charles Wellford Leavitt firm of New York the terraced garden and grounds. The University of Minnesota Duluth runs it as a house museum.',
  'Wikipedia says the furniture brought in during 1908 and 1909 still stands almost where it was first placed. The estate\'s own page describes 12 acres of gardens, bridges and lakefront paths round the house. Tours by ticket; Christmas tours from mid-November.',
  'A mining fortune\'s Gilded Age house with its furniture still in place.',
  accn='University of Minnesota Duluth, 3300 London Road, Duluth (218-726-8910). Tickets on the estate\'s site. Hours from its hours page, read November 20, 2026; it was closed November 2 to 5 and 9 to 12 for Christmas decorating and is closed on Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve and Christmas Day. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Glensheen Historic Estate'),['Glensheen: hours of operation','https://glensheen.org/visit/visitor-information/hours-operation']],
  hours='Oct 20 to Jan 4 daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Jan 5 to Mar 4 Fri to Sun 10 to 4; Mar 5 to May 17 Thu to Mon 10 to 4; May 18 to Oct 19 daily 9 to 5',brick='yes'),
N('jay-cooke-river-inn-swinging-bridge','River Inn and Swinging Bridge, Jay Cooke State Park','Thomson Township','mn','inst','standing',46.65417,-92.37139,'site','public','walk','','1933–1942',
  'The first land of the park was given to the state by the Saint Louis Power Company in 1915. A Civilian Conservation Corps camp set up in 1933 built a rustic swinging bridge over the St. Louis River; it closed in 1935, and a second camp, from 1939 to 1942, rebuilt the bridge and built the River Inn, now the park\'s visitor centre. The record flood of 2012 destroyed the historic bridge, and it was replaced by 2014.',
  'The CCC\'s stone-and-log River Inn and, about 110 m away, the suspension footbridge over the rocky gorge of the St. Louis River, one of two suspension bridges in Minnesota state parks (Wikipedia; the bridge itself is the 2014 replacement). The DNR also points to CCC structures at Oldenburg Point, among them a 1934 water tower and latrine, and the Thomson pioneer cemetery. Interior of the River Inn not checked.',
  'A CCC footbridge over a river gorge, swept away in 2012 and rebuilt.',
  accn='Jay Cooke State Park, Minnesota DNR, on Highway 210 east of Carlton. A state park vehicle permit is required. The DNR says to park at the River Inn for the bridge, a route accessible to all. Park hours from the DNR page, read November 20, 2026; the office and River Inn are typically open 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. when staffed (218-673-7000).',
  links=[W('Jay Cooke State Park'),DNR('jay_cooke','Jay Cooke State Park')],
  hours='Park daily 8 a.m. to 10 p.m.; River Inn usually 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('soudan-underground-mine','Soudan Underground Mine','Breitung Township','mn','industry','standing',47.81924,-92.24167,'site','open','walk','','1882–1962',
  'Iron ore was found here by prospectors looking for gold: rich hematite, often more than 65 percent iron. An open pit opened in 1882 and mining went underground by 1900. U.S. Steel\'s Oliver Iron Mining division owned it from 1901 until mining ended in 1962, when level 27 was being opened at 2,341 feet. U.S. Steel gave the mine to the state in 1965; it is a National Historic Landmark and the heart of Lake Vermilion-Soudan Underground Mine State Park.',
  'The steel headframe over shaft 8 and the surface buildings of the mine, which Wikipedia says are open to the public; underground tours ride the cage down to the 27th level in summer. The DNR also names a West Tower Mine Trail to a boardwalk over the deepest mine pit. Interior not checked.',
  'Minnesota\'s oldest and deepest iron mine, half a mile down.',
  accn='Lake Vermilion-Soudan Underground Mine State Park, Minnesota DNR. A vehicle permit is needed everywhere in the park except the main lot at the mine\'s visitor centre. The DNR warns that GPS units send drivers the wrong way: follow the street signs from Tower. Underground tours resume May 29, 2027 (DNR page, read November 20, 2026); the winter office is at 1379 Stuntz Bay Road.',
  links=[W('Lake Vermilion-Soudan Underground Mine State Park'),DNR('lake_vermilion_soudan','Lake Vermilion-Soudan Underground Mine State Park')],
  hours='Park daily 8 a.m. to 10 p.m.; underground mine tours in summer, resuming May 29, 2027',brick='?',
  th=[47.81910,-92.24383],thn='Mine visitor centre lot, McKinley Park Road (OpenStreetMap)'),
N('kettle-river-sandstone-quarry','Kettle River Sandstone Company Quarry (Robinson Park)','Sandstone','mn','industry','ruins',46.13161,-92.85887,'site','public','walk','','1885–1919',
  'Minnesota\'s largest sandstone quarry of its time, opened in 1885 on the Kettle River, which gave the town its name. Its pink stone went into hundreds of buildings, among them the foundations of the Minneapolis City Hall and Courthouse. The Hinckley fire of September 1, 1894 burned nearly everything in the quarry and the town; it was rebuilt, and at its peak held at least 15 buildings and dozens of derricks. It closed in 1919; its buildings and machinery were destroyed or salvaged in the Second World War, and the city made it a park in 1960 (Register form).',
  'The Register form lists quarry walls of sandstone 75 to 125 feet high with drill marks from blasting, the walls of two stone crushers, a circular sandstone artesian well, the beds of the tracks that moved the blocks, several building foundations, and a derrick mast of about 1900 still on its foundation. Paths have been cleared through the site.',
  'Where the pink stone for a hundred courthouses came from.',
  accn='City-owned Robinson Park on the east edge of Sandstone, along the Kettle River (Register form). Park hours and rules not checked; the city\'s page was not read. A mapped lot on Old Wagon Road, about 170 m northeast of the pin, was not checked on the aerial.',
  links=[NOM('91000877','Register nomination: Kettle River Sandstone Company Quarry (1991)'),W('Sandstone, Minnesota')],
  hours='?',brick='no',th=[46.13284,-92.85762],thn='Lot on Old Wagon Road (OpenStreetMap)'),
# ---- Twin Cities ----
N('historic-fort-snelling','Historic Fort Snelling','Fort Snelling Unorganized Territory','mn','fort','standing',44.89285,-93.18226,'site','open','walk','','1820s–1946',
  'A fort on the bluff where the Minnesota River meets the Mississippi, first called Fort Saint Anthony and renamed when its building was finished in 1825. The Army let officers and fur traders bring enslaved people; Dred Scott and Harriet Robinson Scott lived here in the 1830s, and their later suit for freedom became the Dred Scott case. The fort was a recruiting station from the Civil War to the Second World War and was decommissioned in 1946; the lower post was restored to its old look in 1965.',
  'The round tower (pin, from its OpenStreetMap outline) and the walled lower post, a National Historic Landmark. MNHS says the historic fort is seen by guided walking tour only, weather allowing, from the Plank Museum and visitor centre. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The frontier fort where Dred Scott lived.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society, 200 Tower Avenue, St. Paul. Enter "Historic Fort Snelling" in a GPS, and do not follow signs for Fort Snelling State Park, a different place. Parking $6 a day. Admission $12 for adults. Hours from the MNHS hours page, read November 20, 2026; it lists holiday closings.',
  links=[W('Fort Snelling'),MNHS('fortsnelling/visit/hours','Historic Fort Snelling, hours and admission'),MNHS('fortsnelling/visit/directions','directions and parking')],
  hours='September 7 to December 31, 2026: Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; the fort by guided walking tour only',brick='?'),
N('folsom-house-taylors-falls','Folsom House','Taylors Falls','mn','landmark','standing',45.40063,-92.65409,'exact','open','walk','','1854',
  'The house of the lumberman W. H. C. Folsom, who brought his family to Taylors Falls in 1850 and later sat one term in the state House and six in the Senate. Wikipedia gives it as built in 1854, in a Greek Revival and Federal style. Five generations of the family lived in it; it stands in the Angel\'s Hill Historic District, a village laid out in the New England manner.',
  'MNHS says the house keeps its original style and the family\'s own furnishings, among them a Hews rectangular grand piano, Folsom\'s large library and clothing; it is shown by guided tour.',
  'A lumber baron\'s house with the family\'s piano and books still in it.',
  accn='Run by the Taylors Falls Historical Society with the Minnesota Historical Society, 272 West Government Street (651-465-3125). Admission free, donations welcome. Parking not checked. The MNHS hours page, read November 20, 2026, showed every day closed from October 19 to November 26 and listed 2026 holiday openings of 1 to 4 p.m.; summer hours not read.',
  links=[W('Folsom House'),MNHS('folsomhouse/visit/hours','Folsom House, hours and admission'),MNHS('folsomhouse/activities','Folsom House, what to see')],
  hours='Closed for the season when read in November 2026; 2026 holiday openings (Memorial Day, July 4, Labor Day) 1 to 4 p.m.',brick='some'),
# ---- Minnesota River valley and the southwest ----
N('seppman-mill','Seppman Mill','South Bend Township','mn','industry','standing',44.16389,-94.11333,'site','public','walk','','1862–1890',
  'A stone windmill built by Louis Seppmann, a stonemason from Westphalia, on his own land. He and fellow masons laid the first 10 feet of wall in 1862 before money troubles and the Dakota War stopped them; Wikipedia gives it as finished in 1864 and the Register list as completed in 1863. Seppmann carved most of the wooden machinery himself. In a good wind it ground 150 bushels of wheat a day; it ran until 1890, when a storm destroyed the last of its arms (Register list).',
  'A conical stone tower about 32 feet high, its sails and inner machinery gone (Wikipedia). The Seppmanns first lived in a small stone house beside it, used as a warehouse from the 1870s; whether it survives was not checked.',
  'A stonemason\'s windmill on the prairie, built through a war.',
  accn='Minneopa State Park, Minnesota DNR, 54497 Gadwall Road, Mankato; the mill is off Highway 68 in the park. A state park vehicle permit is required. Park hours from the DNR page, read November 20, 2026. Parking at the mill not checked.',
  links=[W('Seppman Mill'),DNR('minneopa','Minneopa State Park')],
  hours='Park daily 8 a.m. to 10 p.m.',brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('fort-ridgely','Fort Ridgely','Ridgely Township','mn','fort','ruins',44.45339,-94.73478,'site','public','walk','','1853–1867',
  'A frontier Army post of 1851 to 1867, built in 1853 and 1854 above the Minnesota River southwest of Fairfax, with no wall or palisade. Granite for its stone barracks was quarried two miles away by the troops. It held out against attacks in the US-Dakota War of 1862 and was a training ground for Civil War recruits (MNHS).',
  'The stone foundations of eight fort buildings, laid bare by state archaeologists, with markers on each; the old commissary, partly rebuilt by the Civilian Conservation Corps in the 1930s, holds the site\'s exhibits; an 1896 monument and the Fort Ridgely Cemetery next to the grounds (MNHS; Wikipedia). MNHS says the commissary is closed for now.',
  'A fort without walls that held through the 1862 war.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society site inside Fort Ridgely State Park, 72404 County Road 30, Fairfax (507-628-5591). Grounds free; a state park vehicle permit is required. The DNR says the historic site is not part of the state park. Parking is the lot beside the commissary, seen on the aerial. Hours from the MNHS page, read November 20, 2026.',
  links=[W('Fort Ridgely'),MNHS('fortridgely','Fort Ridgely'),MNHS('fortridgely/activities','Fort Ridgely, what to see'),DNR('fort_ridgely','Fort Ridgely State Park')],
  hours='Grounds daily dawn to dusk; commissary building closed when read in November 2026',brick='no',
  th=[44.45351,-94.73536],thn='Lot beside the commissary (from the aerial)'),
N('harkin-store-west-newton','Harkin Store','West Newton Township','mn','civic','standing',44.38696,-94.59916,'exact','public','drive','','1867–1901',
  'All that is left of West Newton. Alexander Harkin opened the store, with the post office, in 1867. The town declined after four years of locust swarms and when the railroad went elsewhere, and the store closed in 1901 with most of its stock left inside; the Minnesota Historical Society keeps it as a museum (Wikipedia).',
  'A general store of the 1870s with the family\'s house joined to it. MNHS says the store building is closed for now and visitors walk round the outside on a self-guided tour; interior not checked.',
  'A general store shut in 1901 with the goods still on the shelves.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society, 66250 County Road 21, northwest of New Ulm. Free. MNHS page read November 20, 2026. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Harkin\'s General Store'),MNHS('harkinstore','Harkin Store'),MNHS('harkinstore/activities','Harkin Store, what to see')],
  hours='Grounds daily dawn to dusk; store building closed when read in November 2026',brick='?'),
N('pipestone-commercial-district','Pipestone Commercial Historic District','Pipestone','mn','town','standing',44.000502,-96.317621,'area','public','walk','','1880s–1890s',
  'Two blocks of downtown Pipestone with about 30 commercial buildings, 18 of them built of Sioux quartzite, most in the 1890s by local tradesmen: the largest group of such buildings in the state, says its 1977 Register form. The dark red stone came from Pipestone\'s own quarries and the light pink, called jasper locally, from nearby Jasper.',
  'Two- and three-storey fronts of dark red quartzite trimmed with pink belt courses, lintels and window arches, several with checkerboard friezes of the two colours. The form names the four-storey Romanesque Calumet Hotel at the main crossroads as the most prominent building, and the block holding the Pipestone County Museum, the Shoe Box, the Masonic Temple and Julian\'s Pastry Shop as the strongest. Interiors not checked.',
  'A downtown built of red and pink quartzite in checkerboard patterns.',
  accn='Public streets of downtown Pipestone, round Main Street and Hiawatha Avenue. The pin is the Register list\'s point for the district. Street parking. Shop and museum hours not checked.',
  links=[NOM('77000761','Register nomination: Pipestone Commercial Historic District (1977)')],
  hours='?',brick='no'),
# ---- Southeast ----
N('wasioja-seminary-ruins','Wasioja Seminary ruins','Wasioja Township','mn','civic','ruins',44.077,-92.81628,'site','public','walk','','1858–1905',
  'The stone shell of the Minnesota Seminary at Wasioja, a frontier village of about 1,000 by 1860 that faded after the Civil War took its men, the railroad passed it by in 1866 and it lost the county seat. Dodge County says the seminary was dedicated in 1858; Wikipedia and a 2026 news report say it opened in 1860. Its students volunteered nearly to a man for the Union and many fell; a fire gutted the building in 1905, leaving the walls (Dodge County).',
  'Stone walls stabilised in 2024 to 2026 with state money; the project was to build a catwalk through the ruins and a fence round them, and the ruins were opened to the public in July 2026 (Dodge County; KTTC). The rest of the village is a Register district: the Baptist church, the school, a Civil War recruiting station, the stone Andrew Doig house and a limestone kiln (Wikipedia).',
  'The walls of a seminary whose students went to war and did not come back.',
  accn='A local park (Wikipedia), stabilised and opened by Dodge County. Mapped lot on 602nd Street about 320 m northwest of the pin, not checked on the aerial. Hours and rules not checked.',
  links=[W('Wasioja, Minnesota'),['Dodge County: $2.1 million grant for Wasioja Seminary Ruins stabilization','https://dodgecountymn.gov/news_detail_T32_R626.php'],['KTTC (July 28, 2026): Community marks completion of Wasioja Seminary Ruins Stabilization Project','https://www.kttc.com/2026/07/28/community-marks-completion-wasioja-seminary-ruins-stabilization-project/']],
  hours='?',brick='?',conf='mixed',th=[44.07873,-92.81946],thn='Lot on 602nd Street (OpenStreetMap)'),
N('dodge-county-courthouse-mantorville','Dodge County Courthouse','Mantorville','mn','civic','standing',44.06839,-92.75496,'exact','open','walk','','1865',
  'Designed by E. Townsend Mix and built in 1865 of the soft local Mantorville limestone, which hardens as it weathers. Wikipedia calls it the oldest working courthouse in Minnesota. It stands in the Mantorville Historic District, 24 buildings of 1854 to 1918 that include the Hubbell House hotel.',
  'A limestone courthouse in a village centre of the same stone. Interior not checked.',
  'Minnesota\'s oldest courthouse still at work.',
  accn='Dodge County, on North Main Street in Mantorville. The county\'s page, read November 20, 2026, gives county office hours; it does not say which offices are in this building. Street parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Dodge County Courthouse (Minnesota)'),W('Mantorville, Minnesota'),['Dodge County','https://www.co.dodge.mn.us/']],
  hours='County offices Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('pickwick-mill','Pickwick Mill','Pickwick','mn','industry','standing',43.98029,-91.49665,'exact','open','drive','','1854',
  'Built on Big Trout Creek by Thomas Grant and Wilson Davis; its Register form says in 1854, as a grist and saw mill, turned to flour in 1856. It is one of southeast Minnesota\'s oldest water-powered mills. A tornado in 1907 tore off the roof and top storey, which were replaced by a flat plank roof; a pitched roof has since been put back. It is now a milling museum.',
  'A six-storey mill of local limestone with a timber frame built without nails, turned by a 20-foot water wheel (Wikipedia). Shown by its volunteers in season.',
  'A six-storey stone mill with a frame held together without a nail.',
  accn='Pickwick Mill, Inc., 24813 County Road 7, Winona (507-457-0499). Wikipedia says tours run May to October; the mill\'s own 2026 hours post, read November 20, 2026, gives the days. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Pickwick Mill'),['Pickwick Mill','https://pickwickmill.org/']],
  hours='May to October; 2026 season from May 22: Wednesday to Sunday noon to 5 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('historic-forestville','Historic Forestville','Forestville Township','mn','town','standing',43.64294,-92.21479,'site','open','walk','','1853–1910',
  'Settled in 1853 and platted in 1854, Forestville had more than 150 people by 1860. The railroad passed it by in 1868, and the Meighen family, who kept the store, bought up the town and land round it through foreclosures until they owned it all by 1889 and paid their workers mostly in store credit. The state authorised Forestville State Park in 1949 to keep what was left of the townsite (MNHS; Wikipedia).',
  'The brick store and house the Meighens finished in 1857 beside the site of the Fremont House hotel (MNHS). The 1899 bridge over the Root River was moved off County Road 118 in November 2023 to be repaired, and is to go back to its old place (MNHS). Interior and what else stands not checked.',
  'A company town one family owned outright, then left.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society site inside Forestville/Mystery Cave State Park, 21899 County Road 118, Preston. Free; a state park vehicle permit is required. The MNHS page gives grounds hours only and no times for the store. A mapped lot on County Road 118, about 190 m south, was not checked on the aerial. Pages read November 20, 2026.',
  links=[W('Forestville, Minnesota'),MNHS('forestville/visit/hours','Historic Forestville, hours'),MNHS('forestville/learn/site','About Forestville'),MNHS('forestville/learn/bridge','Historic bridge')],
  hours='Grounds daily dawn to dusk',brick='?',th=[43.64129,-92.21530],thn='Lot on County Road 118 (OpenStreetMap)'),
# ---- Red River valley ----
N('comstock-house-moorhead','Comstock House','Moorhead','mn','landmark','standing',46.86903,-96.76758,'exact','open','walk','','1882–1883',
  'The house of Solomon Comstock, one of Moorhead\'s first settlers and a figure in its business, politics and schools. Begun in July 1882 to a design by Kees and Fisk, it mixes Queen Anne and Eastlake styles and cost $45,000, the dearest on its block; Comstock lived in it 51 years and his wife Sarah 59.',
  'Eleven rooms, each finished in a different wood with its own wallpaper; solid oak doors and windows, a butternut stair rail and three butternut mantels, a parquet border in the dining room, carved door knobs, spindled porches and tall patterned chimneys (MNHS). Shown by a guided tour of both floors.',
  'An 1880s house with a different wood in every room.',
  accn='Run by the Minnesota Historical Society with the Historical and Cultural Society of Clay County, 506 Eighth Street South, on Highway 75 at Fifth Avenue South (218-291-4211). Free parking on 5th Avenue. Admission $8 for adults. The MNHS hours page, read November 20, 2026, showed every day closed from August 23 to December 31, 2026; summer hours not read.',
  links=[W('Comstock House'),MNHS('comstock/visit/hours','Comstock House, hours and admission'),MNHS('comstock/learn/house','About the house')],
  hours='Closed August 23 to December 31, 2026; when open, tours on the hour, about an hour long, last at 3 p.m.',brick='?'),
]
